dict(pas="14.18", w="子貢=zǐ gòng/Zigong | 曰：「/said | ^管仲=guǎn zhòng/Guan Zhong | 非/(was) not | 仁/humane | 者/one (who is) | 與？=yú/(was he?) | ^桓公=huán gōng/Duke Huan//Huan (his posthumous name)//duke | 殺=shā/killed | 公子糾，=gōng zǐ jiū/Prince Jiu//duke's//son//Jiu (his personal name) | ^不/(he) could not | 能/(bring himself) | 死，/to die | ^又/and moreover | 相=xiàng/assisted | 之。」/him",
  l="Zigong said: “Guan Zhong was not a humane one, was he?\nDuke Huan killed Prince Jiu;\nhe could not die,\nand moreover he assisted him.”",
  i="Zigong said: “Surely Guan Zhong was not a humane man? When Duke Huan had Prince Jiu put to death, Guan Zhong could not die with his master — and then he went on to serve Duke Huan as his minister.”",
  n="Zigong raises the doubt Zilu raised in 14.17. 管仲 Guan Zhong (3.22) first served 公子糾 Prince Jiu of Qi; when Jiu's brother 桓公 Duke Huan (r. 685–643 BC) took the throne, Jiu was killed, Shao Hu, who had served Jiu with him, died with him, and Guan Zhong, recommended by Bao Shuya, became Huan's chief minister (the story is in Zhu Xi's note to 14.17). 糾 jiū (Zhu Xi at 14.17: 居黝反; modern jiū). 非仁者與 'not a humane one, was he?': 與 yú is a final question particle (Zhu Xi: 平聲) that asks for agreement. 殺 shā 'to kill'. 不能死 'could not die' — did not die for his lord. 相 xiàng 'to assist', to serve as minister (Zhu Xi: 去聲); 之 'him' is Duke Huan. Zhu Xi: Zigong meant that not dying might still pass, but to go on and serve as Huan's minister was too much (子貢意不死猶可，相之則已甚矣)."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^管仲=guǎn zhòng/Guan Zhong | 相=xiàng/assisting | 桓公，=huán gōng/Duke Huan//Huan (his posthumous name)//duke | ^霸=bà/made (him) head (of) | 諸侯，=zhū hóu/the feudal lords | ^一/once (and for all) | 匡/set right | 天下，=tiān xià/the world | ^民/the people | 到=dào/down | 于/to | 今/now | 受/receive | 其/his | 賜。/bounty | ^微/were it not for | 管仲，=guǎn zhòng/Guan Zhong | ^吾/we | 其/surely (would) | 被=pī/let hang loose | 髮=fà/(our) hair | 左/(and) on the left | 衽=rèn/(fold our) robes | 矣。」/(so it would be)",
  l="The Master said: “Guan Zhong, assisting Duke Huan,\nmade him head of the feudal lords,\nonce and for all set right the world;\nthe people down to now receive his bounty.\nWere it not for Guan Zhong,\nwe would surely let our hair hang loose and fold our robes on the left.”",
  i="The Master said: “As Duke Huan's minister, Guan Zhong made him leader of all the lords and set the whole world straight at a stroke; people are still enjoying the benefit today. Had there been no Guan Zhong, we should all have our hair hanging loose and our robes fastened on the left, like the barbarians.”",
  n="相 xiàng 'to assist' (as unit 23). 霸 bà: Zhu Xi: 霸 is the same word as 伯 'eldest', and means 'to be head' (霸與伯同，長也) — with Guan Zhong's help Duke Huan became the first of the hegemons, the leaders of the lords. 匡 kuāng 'to set right' (Zhu Xi and Ma Rong: 匡，正也); 一 'once, at one stroke'. Zhu Xi: honouring the house of Zhou and driving back the Yi and Di tribes were both ways of setting the world right. Ma Rong (in He Yan): the Son of Heaven was weak, and Duke Huan led the lords in honouring the house of Zhou, setting the whole world right at once. 到于今 'reaching to now': 于 = 於. 賜 'what is bestowed, a bounty'. 微 'were it not for' (Zhu Xi and Ma Rong: 微，無也). 其 before the verb: 'surely, would'. 被 pī 'to let hang loose' (Zhu Xi: 皮寄反; modern pī); 髮 fà 'the hair of the head'; 衽 rèn 'the front flap of a robe' (Zhu Xi: 衣衿也; 而審反), and 左衽 is closing it on the left side. Zhu Xi: loose hair and robes closed on the left are the customs of the Yi and Di. He Yan's own comment: 'receive his bounty' means the boon of not wearing loose hair and left-closed robes; Ma Rong: without Guan Zhong, lords would not be lords nor ministers ministers, and all would have become Yi and Di. The Master's answer runs on past Zhu Xi's comment; the next unit is left unquoted."),
dict(w="豈/how would (he) | 若/be like | 匹夫=pǐ fū/common men//single//man | 匹婦=pǐ fù/(and) common women//single//woman | 之/'s | 為=wéi/practising | 諒=liàng/petty good faith | 也，/(pause) | ^自/themselves | 經=jīng/hanging | 於/in | 溝/a ditch | 瀆=dú/(or) a drain | 而/and | 莫/no one | 之/them | 知=zhī/knowing (of) | 也？/(question)",
  l="How would he be like common men and common women keeping their petty good faith,\nhanging themselves in a ditch or a drain and no one knowing of it?",
  i="Would you have had him be like some common man or woman who, to keep a small promise, strangles himself in a ditch, and nobody ever hears of it?",
  n="Still the Master, unquoted. 豈若…也 'how would he be like…?', a rhetorical question. 匹夫匹婦 'a single man, a single woman': ordinary people of no standing (匹夫 'a lone commoner' at 9.25). 之 makes 'common men and women practising petty good faith' one phrase. 諒 liàng: Zhu Xi: 諒，小信也 'small good faith', keeping one's word in a small matter. 經 jīng is here 'to hang oneself' (Zhu Xi: 經，縊也). 溝 'a ditch', 瀆 dú 'a drain, a channel'. 莫之知 'no one knows of them': the pronoun 之 stands before the verb after 莫. Zhu Xi: 莫之知 means others do not know; the History of the Later Han, quoting this, has 人 before 莫. He quotes Master Cheng: Duke Huan was the elder brother and Jiu the younger; Guan Zhong, privately helping the man he served to contend for the state, was not doing right; Huan went too far in killing Jiu, yet Jiu's death was his due. Having plotted with him, Guan Zhong might have died with him; knowing that helping him contend was not right, he might also save himself to seek later achievement — so the sage did not blame him for not dying and praised what he achieved. Had Huan been the younger and Jiu the elder, Huan would have been an enemy Guan Zhong could not live under the same sky with, and for the sage to weigh his later achievement and approve his serving Huan would have done great harm to what is right and opened the way to faithless turncoats for ten thousand ages — like Wang Gui and Wei Zheng of the Tang, who did not die with Jiancheng and went over to Taizong: whatever they later achieved, how could it redeem that? Zhu Xi adds his own view (愚謂): Guan Zhong had achievement and no guilt, so the sage praised only his achievement; Wang and Wei had guilt first and achievement after, and one may rightly refuse to let the one cover the other. Wang Su (in He Yan): 經 is dying by hanging, in a ditch; the comment goes on that between Guan Zhong and Shao Hu and Prince Jiu the bond of lord and minister was not yet fully set, so dying for him deserved no deep praise and not dying no great blame — hence the Master praised only Guan Zhong's achievement, and did not say Shao Hu was wrong to die."),
